{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.02718","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.02718","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.02718","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangzhou","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Fangzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sandeep","familyName":"Silwal","name":"Silwal, Sandeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient Training-Free Online Routing for High-Volume Multi-LLM Serving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-02T18:15:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-08T00:47:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-20T22:47:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-22T00:16:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-12T21:16:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-16T01:09:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Increasing demand for Large Language Models (LLMs) services imposes substantial deployment and computation costs on providers. LLM routing offers a cost-efficient solution by directing queries to the optimal LLM based on model and query features. However, existing works primarily focus on offline scenarios and struggle to adapt to online settings with high query volume and constrained token budgets. In this work, we introduce the first training-free algorithm for online routing scenarios. Our algorithm leverages approximate nearest neighbor search to efficiently estimate query features and performs a one-time optimization over a small set of initial queries to learn a routing strategy that guides future routing. We provide theoretical guarantees demonstrating that our algorithm achieves a competitive ratio of $1 - o(1)$ under natural assumptions, which is further validated by extensive experiments across 3 benchmark datasets and 8 baselines, showing an average improvement of 3.55$\\times$ in overall performance, 1.85$\\times$ in cost efficiency, and nearly 4.25$\\times$ in throughput. Our code is available at https://github.com/fzwark/PORT."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.02718","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-04T01:32:47Z","registered":"2025-09-04T01:32:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-16T04:48:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.01920","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.01920","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.01920","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yilin","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Yilin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingfeng","familyName":"Lan","name":"Lan, Qingfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sun","familyName":"Fei","name":"Fei, Sun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dujian","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Dujian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Devang","familyName":"Acharya","name":"Acharya, Devang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William Yang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, William Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenyue","familyName":"Hua","name":"Hua, Wenyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dynamic Speculative Agent Planning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-02T03:34:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-03T01:46:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-05T04:49:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-08T00:20:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-21T01:40:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:43:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite their remarkable success in complex tasks propelling widespread adoption, large language-model-based agents still face critical deployment challenges due to prohibitive latency and inference costs. While recent work has explored various methods to accelerate inference, existing approaches suffer from significant limitations: they either fail to preserve performance fidelity, require extensive offline training of router modules, or incur excessive operational costs. Moreover, they provide minimal user control over the tradeoff between acceleration and other performance metrics. To address these gaps, we introduce Dynamic Speculative Planning (DSP), an asynchronous online reinforcement learning framework that provides lossless acceleration with substantially reduced costs without requiring additional pre-deployment preparation. DSP explicitly optimizes a joint objective balancing end-to-end latency against dollar cost, allowing practitioners to adjust a single parameter that steers the system toward faster responses, cheaper operation, or any point along this continuum. Experiments on two standard agent benchmarks demonstrate that DSP achieves comparable efficiency to the fastest lossless acceleration method while reducing total cost by 30% and unnecessary cost up to 60%. Our code and data are available through https://github.com/guanyilin428/Dynamic-Speculative-Planning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages, 11 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.01920","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-03T04:08:20Z","registered":"2025-09-03T04:08:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:50:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.03047","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.03047","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.03047","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haijun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Haijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinxiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jinxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenhua","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Zhenhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanyong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yanyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuejie","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Xuejie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaining","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Kaining","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaqing","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yaqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ting","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Jie","name":"Jie, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiemin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Xiemin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhifang","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Zhifang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhua","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Junhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuwei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shuwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoming","familyName":"Bao","name":"Bao, Xiaoming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hua","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Hua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shixiong","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Shixiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongwei","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Hongwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ziyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunsheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chunsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FlashRecovery: Fast and Low-Cost Recovery from Failures for Large-Scale Training of LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-03T06:19:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-04T00:24:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have made a profound impact across various fields due to their advanced capabilities. However, training these models at unprecedented scales requires extensive AI accelerator clusters and sophisticated parallelism strategies, which pose significant challenges in maintaining system reliability over prolonged training periods. A major concern is the substantial loss of training time caused by inevitable hardware and software failures. To address these challenges, we present FlashRecovery, a fast and low-cost failure recovery system comprising three core modules: (1) Active and real-time failure detection. This module performs continuous training state monitoring, enabling immediate identification of hardware and software failures within seconds, thus ensuring rapid incident response; (2) Scale-independent task restart. By employing different recovery strategies for normal and faulty nodes, combined with an optimized communication group reconstruction protocol, our approach ensures that the recovery time remains nearly constant, regardless of cluster scale; (3) Checkpoint-free recovery within one step. Our novel recovery mechanism enables single-step restoration, completely eliminating dependence on traditional checkpointing methods and their associated overhead. Collectively, these innovations enable FlashRecovery to achieve optimal Recovery Time Objective (RTO) and Recovery Point Objective (RPO), substantially improving the reliability and efficiency of long-duration LLM training. Experimental results demonstrate that FlashRecovery system can achieve training restoration on training cluster with 4, 800 devices in 150 seconds. We also verify that the time required for failure recovery is nearly consistent for different scales of training tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.03047","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-04T01:40:33Z","registered":"2025-09-04T01:40:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-04T01:40:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":3,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=doi%3a%2810.48550%2farXiv.2509.02718+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2509.03047+OR+10.48550%2farXiv.2509.01920%29\u0026page[size]=100"}}